WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.850
Task statement 5.4.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:05.902
Manage context effectively in large codebase exploration.

3
00:00:05.902 --> 00:00:15.711
Two hours into mapping a legacy monolith, a long session starts describing 'typical' patterns instead of what this codebase actually does.

4
00:00:17.111 --> 00:00:20.786
Exploring a large codebase fills the context window quickly.

5
00:00:20.786 --> 00:00:27.094
As it fills, the model's answers slide from what this code actually does toward what code usually does.

6
00:00:27.094 --> 00:00:31.014
Early, specific findings get crowded out by later file contents.

7
00:00:31.014 --> 00:00:36.097
It is like a notebook so full that you stop checking your notes and start guessing.

8
00:00:37.497 --> 00:00:39.865
Move findings out of the conversation.

9
00:00:39.865 --> 00:00:42.856
Keep a scratchpad file of what you have learned.

10
00:00:42.856 --> 00:00:49.211
Hand focused questions to subagents, which explore in their own context and send back a short summary.

11
00:00:49.211 --> 00:00:53.261
And maintain a manifest of what is done, in progress and pending.

12
00:00:53.261 --> 00:00:58.681
If the session crashes or compacts, the manifest lets you resume without starting over.

13
00:01:00.081 --> 00:01:01.828
Treat exploration in phases.

14
00:01:01.828 --> 00:01:06.694
Map the structure, investigate one area, write the summary down, then move on.

15
00:01:06.694 --> 00:01:13.743
When discovery output has filled the context, run compact, and give it a focus so the summary keeps what matters.

16
00:01:13.743 --> 00:01:23.911
Current docs note that a plan file is re-injected after compaction, while conversation detail is summarised, which is another reason to keep key findings in files.

17
00:01:25.311 --> 00:01:28.590
Here is how that looks on a large legacy codebase.

18
00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.804
First, map the top-level modules into a manifest.

19
00:01:31.804 --> 00:01:37.379
Then send one subagent per specific question, such as who writes to the ledger table.

20
00:01:37.379 --> 00:01:39.872
Each summary goes into the scratchpad.

21
00:01:39.872 --> 00:01:43.741
Between phases, compact with a focus on the open questions.

22
00:01:43.741 --> 00:01:46.431
The main session stays light and precise.

23
00:01:47.831 --> 00:01:53.580
The exam's wrong answers usually keep everything in one long session or delegate vaguely.

24
00:01:53.580 --> 00:02:06.305
The right answers persist findings to files, ask subagents precise questions, keep a manifest for recovery, and compact deliberately with a focus, instead of hoping the automatic pass guesses well.

25
00:02:07.705 --> 00:02:08.301
To recap.

26
00:02:08.301 --> 00:02:14.056
Large explorations degrade as context fills, so keep findings outside the conversation.

27
00:02:14.056 --> 00:02:25.433
Use subagents for specific questions, keep a manifest for recovery, summarise between phases, and use compact deliberately when discovery output starts to crowd the window.

